diff --git a/docs/source/mp/configuration.rst b/docs/source/mp/configuration.rst
index dfc98fe8..51187fa8 100644
--- a/docs/source/mp/configuration.rst
+++ b/docs/source/mp/configuration.rst
@@ -471,6 +471,47 @@ Source: ``lmcache/v1/distributed/config.py``
      - ``0.2``
      - Fraction of allocated memory to evict when triggered (0.0--1.0).
 
+Prefix Pinning
+--------------
+
+Source: ``lmcache/v1/multiprocess/config.py``, ``lmcache/v1/distributed/config.py``
+
+A pin group holds one conversation prefix resident so it survives the idle gap
+between two turns. LRU cannot do that on its own: its horizon is how much
+unrelated traffic arrives, not when the prefix is reused. Groups are named
+``<model version>:<tail chunk hash>`` and derived from the request, so no client
+has to identify its own trajectory. A longer prefix supersedes its own ancestor,
+so the next turn releases the previous group with no extra call.
+
+.. list-table::
+   :header-rows: 1
+   :widths: 30 15 55
+
+   * - Argument
+     - Default
+     - Description
+   * - ``--l1-pin-policy``
+     - ``off``
+     - Which prefixes to pin. ``off`` disables pinning. ``all`` pins every
+       stored prefix. ``tagged`` pins only requests carrying an
+       ``lmcache.pin_group`` request config. Pinning trades cache capacity for
+       hit rate, so ``all`` is worth enabling only when a prefix is measured to
+       be evicted before its next turn.
+   * - ``--l1-pin-budget-ratio``
+     - ``0.25``
+     - Fraction of ``--l1-size-gb`` that pin groups may hold. Pinned objects
+       cannot be evicted, so this is what keeps pinning from starving ordinary
+       caching. When the budget is exceeded the least recently pinned groups
+       are released, and a single prefix larger than the whole budget stays
+       unpinned rather than overshooting the ceiling.
+   * - ``--l1-pin-ttl-seconds``
+     - ``600.0``
+     - Idle seconds after which a group is released. This is the safety net for
+       a client that never releases, since pins carry no TTL of their own.
+
+``--l1-pin-policy`` must be ``off`` when ``--eviction-policy noop`` is set,
+because pinned objects cannot be reclaimed without eviction.
+
 L2 Policies
 -----------
 
